### Selecting the best model

Define a defensible model-selection strategy before looking at final test results.

Compare models using training cross-validation and validation performance, emphasizing:

- The ability to detect actual failures.
- Precision-recall performance on the minority class.
- A reasonable trade-off between false negatives and false positives.
- Generalization between training, cross-validation, and validation.
- Probability quality and calibration where relevant.
- Model complexity, inference speed, stability, and deployment practicality.

Do not select a model merely because it has the highest accuracy, ROC-AUC, or a single favorable validation score.

Choose a primary selection criterion appropriate to predictive maintenance and explain why it fits the use case. Use average precision as an important ranking metric, with failure recall and false-alarm burden considered explicitly.

If two models have similar predictive performance, prefer the simpler, more stable, easier-to-deploy model unless the evidence supports the more complex alternative.

Do not claim that a model is universally optimal. State that it is the best candidate among the tested models under the chosen evaluation criteria.

## 7. Overfitting prevention and generalization

Treat overfitting prevention as a core requirement, not an optional enhancement.

Implement and document the following:

- Proper train/validation/test separation.
- Stratified cross-validation confined to the training set.
- Pipelines that prevent preprocessing leakage.
- Reasonable hyperparameter ranges and model complexity.
- Regularization where appropriate.
- Tree-depth, leaf-size, and related complexity controls where applicable.
- Early stopping where supported and appropriate.
- Comparisons between training, cross-validation, and validation scores.
- Generalization-gap analysis.
- Checks for suspiciously perfect performance or unusually large metric jumps.
- Sensible probability calibration checks if probabilities will be interpreted operationally.

For tree-based models, test whether depth and minimum-leaf constraints improve generalization. For linear models, use appropriate regularization. Do not use every technique on every algorithm without justification.

Do not deliberately force low scores, but do not hide poor generalization either. If a model overfits, report it, investigate it, and prefer a better-generalizing alternative.

Avoid claiming that overfitting has been eliminated. Explain what evidence supports the conclusion that the chosen model generalizes reasonably well.

If the dataset is too small to establish strong real-world reliability, state that clearly.

# 8. Decision-threshold optimization

A default threshold of 0.5 is not necessarily appropriate for machine failure detection.

For each competitive model, evaluate a reasonable range of probability thresholds using **validation data only**.

Produce a threshold-analysis table with:

- Threshold.
- Precision.
- Recall.
- F1-score.
- False-positive count or rate.
- False-negative count or rate.

Choose a final threshold using a documented operational objective.

For example, predictive maintenance may prioritize catching failures, but a threshold that flags almost every machine is not useful. Balance missed failures against unnecessary maintenance alerts.

Make the selection rule explicit. If no operational costs are provided, use a reasonable, clearly documented default objective, such as a strong recall-oriented precision-recall trade-off subject to an acceptable precision level when the validation data permits it. If no such constraint can be met, explain the trade-off instead of inventing one.

Do not tune the threshold on the test set.

Save the selected threshold and its rationale with the final model artifacts. Allow the dashboard to display the threshold and explain that different maintenance operations may need different settings.

